🟣 Topic 4: Terraform Modules (Theory)
🧠 What is a Module?

👉 A module = reusable Terraform code block

Instead of writing everything in one file:

❌ messy, duplicate code
✅ split into reusable components
🔹 Simple Definition

A module is a container of resources used together

🏗️ Why Modules?
❌ Without Modules
VPC code repeated 5 times
EC2 code repeated 5 times
Hard to maintain ❌
✅ With Modules
Write once → reuse everywhere
Clean structure ✅
📁 Module Structure
module-name/
 ├── main.tf       → resources
 ├── variables.tf  → inputs
 ├── outputs.tf    → outputs
🔄 How Modules Work
ROOT MODULE
     │
     ▼
calls child module
     │
     ▼
MODULE creates resources
     │
     ▼
returns outputs
🔧 Types of Modules
1. Root Module
Main project (your main.tf)
2. Child Module
Custom modules you created:
VPC
NLB
EKS
3. Public Modules
From Terraform Registry

Example:

source = "terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws"
🔥 Key Concepts
🔹 1. Input Variables (Parameterization)

👉 Pass values to module

module "vpc" {
  source = "./modules/vpc"
  vpc_cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"
}
🔹 2. Outputs (Return Values)

👉 Module gives output back

output "vpc_id" {
  value = aws_vpc.main.id
}
🔹 3. Reusability

👉 Same module → multiple environments

module "vpc_dev" { ... }
module "vpc_prod" { ... }
🔹 4. Abstraction

👉 Hide complexity

User only sees:

module "vpc" {
  vpc_cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"
}
